  "preamble": "The source here is vocalic (syllabic) r̩, which has no separate atlas symbol and is filed under r, just as l_to_u files syllabic l̩. In Middle Indo-Aryan the Old Indo-Aryan vowel ṛ [r̩] was lost and replaced by a plain vowel a, i or u. The u outcome is conditioned: it appears when the following syllable contains u (Sanskrit ṛtu > Pali utu, ṛju > uju) or next to a labial consonant (pṛcchati > pucchati, vṛddha > vuḍḍha, pṛthivī > Maharastri puhavī), while elsewhere ṛ usually gives a or i (kṛta > kata, ṛṣi > isi). The same u reflexes appear in the Prakrits and in the Dardic language Kalasha (phúčik 'to ask').",
  "phoneticEffects": "Vocalization, Vowel Harmony, Labial Assimilation",
